WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:22.740
Thank you so much for having me! I threw
out my script too just because I feel

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.279
like everything that I started writing
out everyone was already saying in this

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:33.270
room so I'll just start with I used to
be a scientist for seven years. I did a

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:39.180
lot of excavations out in Spain where
once every month we would open the doors

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:44.940
towards the end of the term to be able
to invite communities from outside

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.239
families to spend their Sunday just like
watching archeologist, paleontologists,

00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:56.280
geologists, paleoanthropologists working
together sort of like a human zoo. And

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.720
there was one time early on in my
SciComm career where I was still a

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.379
scientist, I hadn't been trained in
science communication at that time, where

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:10.200
I decided well actually the director was
like oh I have two foreigners who you

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:16.170
know speak Spanish well enough so
they'll lead the tour for everybody else.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.710
I was really nervous because
obviously I didn't have the right kind

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:24.450
of training to be able to properly
translate why our research matters to

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.930
the people that are coming in, who are
people from Madrid families from

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:34.560
neighboring small towns and so I'm going
through the first field site and this

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.000
wonderful multi-generational family of
grandparents, little grandchildren

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.270
running around, and their parents came
with me.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:47.340
And as I'm talking about one of the
field sites the old man with the cane

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:54.390
comes up to me and he's just like "okay
pero porque me quitandes todo este de

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:58.859
mis memorias? Why are you taking away
all of my memories? I grew up in this

00:01:58.859 --> 00:02:02.340
town and all you're doing is digging
away the hills that my children should

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.499
be playing with that, my grandchildren
should be playing in" and I don't know

00:02:07.499 --> 00:02:14.130
about you but I'm a Filipino
so to have a descendent of my colonizers

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:20.940
who had 300 years erased our culture
from the indigenous indigenous people in

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:29.569
my country to then ask me how it feels
to have their culture taken away.

00:02:29.659 --> 00:02:38.450
I'm sorry was my answer because my
answer was tied to sympathy and empathy

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:46.379
and I was also so aware that I'm not the
right person to be talking to you about

00:02:46.379 --> 00:02:52.579
why our research is destructive but is
valuable for you as well.

00:02:52.579 --> 00:02:59.760
That was my privilege and that was my
own awareness of ok for this moment

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.349
I can at least open the discussion to
beyond these are the benefits that

00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:09.780
you're getting from doing research in
your country in your background in your

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:15.329
backyard. Even though we're destroying it
I can at least talk to you about the

00:03:15.329 --> 00:03:20.010
true nature of science inquiry. But yes
so much of it has been destructive and a

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:24.000
lot of it tied to people of color, to
minorities, to people who've been

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.579
oppressed for years. J Marion Sims and
gynecology and the history of torturing

00:03:29.579 --> 00:03:36.239
black slaves to be able to benefit the
progress of science right? But you don't

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:40.889
often get a chance to share that and so
in that moment I turned it into a

00:03:40.889 --> 00:03:46.169
conversation about the process of
science because that's what he needed. He

00:03:46.169 --> 00:03:50.699
didn't need me to tell him that what we
were doing was somehow beneficial

00:03:50.699 --> 00:03:54.239
because these things are the things that
we were finding are gonna end up in

00:03:54.239 --> 00:04:02.310
their Museum and so that's my first
example of really actively listening to

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:06.000
the people that you're supposed to be
doing science outreach for. Whether or

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.099
not they're the community that you're
going to in their spaces just because

00:04:11.099 --> 00:04:15.359
you as a person go into a new space
doesn't mean you're actually connecting

00:04:15.359 --> 00:04:20.549
with them and I see this time and again
over and over.

00:04:20.549 --> 00:04:25.919
You see scientists who you know need
different ways to be able to get to a

00:04:25.919 --> 00:04:30.960
point where they are adaptable enough no
matter who they're talking to just get

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.210
thrown into a new space that they're not
prepared for and then end up giving the

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:40.510
speech or the lecture that they've just
prepared without being able to

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:45.550
truly listen to the people in front of
them and it's a missed opportunity to be

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:50.650
able to build back and forth. The
difference between what people think

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:53.950
about what dialogue is where you're kind
of just talking and letting people ask

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:59.170
questions is not the same as you as a
person in power asking and conversing

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:03.730
with the other person on the other side
and actually wanting to learn from them

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:09.400
I kind of wonder how many people in the
room who have job opportunities, funding

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:15.640
opportunities, opportunities for someone
to do outreach with your people actually

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:20.650
ask the people in the communities that
you're gonna do it for so your target

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:26.100
audience do any how many people actually
involve them in the creation of the

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:33.690
content that you're in the structure
that you're making? Great, mostly women.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:39.970
And I have to say too that like I kind
of wish that I had the adaptable

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:43.870
training like I as someone who used to
be a scientist is now a science

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:48.430
communicator but also heavily dabbles in
comedy both in improv and standup

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:54.930
outside of my silos of academia I have
learned so much more from people outside

00:05:54.930 --> 00:06:02.110
with similar problems than I have in
academia trying to solve the science

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:06.940
outreach questions that I keep asking
especially tied to diversity and

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:13.570
inclusivity. It's so important to get
out of those spaces because sometimes

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:17.200
the solution to your problem someone
else is already asking somewhere else

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:24.310
and finding a solution for but we keep
finding ourselves in the same circles of

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:29.410
work where you're only tapping into the
network that you're aware of instead of

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:35.770
maybe asking the other diverse people in
the room you I know you I know you so

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:42.070
well that I know there might be someone
else in your community outside of the

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:47.170
sciences, the academia, outside of science
outreach practitioning that would be

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:53.110
beneficial for this. So my example would
be the queer attraction lab that

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:58.900
I produced for guerrilla science,
specifically for a diverse group of

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:05.320
queer folk and I structured it so that I
knew I had a barrier to being able to

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:10.390
put this up because I needed to gain the
trust of queer people so that means I'm

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:14.920
not the right person to be standing in
front of those people to tell them this

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:19.360
is how science applies to your dating
and love lives especially when so much

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:25.120
of science have erased them from
research right. So I structured it so

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:30.580
that the first event that I ran was a
hetero version of attraction lab that

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:35.800
was split into two parts: one section was
for the daters, another section was for

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:40.300
any couple or someone who brings in
their partner and even though the idea

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:45.010
was that we were bringing in a hetero
community so many of the couples and

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:50.550
partners that were in that space where
queer folk who were like this is amazing,

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:55.840
this completely applies to me and I
would love to have one that's just for

00:07:55.840 --> 00:08:00.340
the community and then I took those
learnings and built it to the next one

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:06.460
so that I knew that my my hosts and my
scientists had to be queer people. So I

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:12.970
ended up having two trans folk who knew how to speak to their community and who knew exactly

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:17.770
how the science goes into everyone's
everyday lives in a way that they're not

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:24.370
aware of. But likewise it also opened it
up to the painful conversation of why

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:29.320
people who are asexual aren't even
studied and researched so do they even

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:34.900
exist in science? People who don't
normally get and you know get questions

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:39.820
asked about them and so it opened the
conversation more beyond just how sexy

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:46.660
and fun science can be for everybody but
also how the scientific inquiry can be

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:51.220
changed if more diverse people can be in
it and that anyone can also apply it to

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:58.840
their lives. Time? Okay that's my time.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:14.820
Anybody? Oh sorry.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:16.980
Hi! My name is Helen Chang. I'm from New York

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:22.920
Sea Grant and I had a question about
that was a great presentation I really

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:28.140
enjoyed it. I had a question posed to all
the present presenters. So I feel like a

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:33.750
lot of people are really ingrained in
their ways and their behaviors and so

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:38.700
there's this wall of distrust especially
towards the science community and I

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:45.360
wonder how we can sort of break down
those walls so that this trust is

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:53.870
reestablished and that there that
science is you know a trusted source.

00:09:56.270 --> 00:10:01.710
The people who run focus groups, do you
train up your facilitators to recognize

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:05.970
when people start using their power over
other speakers so that you can lessen

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:09.870
that whole idea of I'm the expert and
then you know just listen to me because

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:15.060
I have the answers to what you're saying
or you know are trying to recognize in

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:18.690
those moments where you're creating a
space for true communication between

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:24.630
people with different powers knowledge
bases where you can convince the experts

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:30.990
in the room that like lay persons
are also experts in a way that involve

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:36.690
their you know they inform their own
research because those moments are easy

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:42.980
to do on a one-on-one especially if
you've trained yourself enough to be

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:49.040
aware of your own biases and your own
privilege right? At least in my

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:52.500
personal experience for example at
guerrilla science I'm really great for

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:58.470
grateful for someone like Marc who's my
boss who in a space where producers are

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:03.089
pitching ideas he sees my face start to
crunch up because I don't notice it but

00:11:03.089 --> 00:11:06.910
it's a gut reaction for me as a person
of color being like ooh that sounds so

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:12.970
not good and then encouraging me enough
to be like your voice matters you know

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:18.550
share it share why share why you're
feeling so uncomfortable right now. So I

00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:21.960
don't know any of the other presenters.

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:44.340
I was just gonna follow up on that; do
you have examples of that type of

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:48.420
training that we can seek out or places
where it's publicly available or if you

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:50.700
guys have it is it publicly available at
all?

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:55.740
For facilitators you mean? Well I did my
masters in science communication and

00:11:55.740 --> 00:12:01.790
public engagement at the University of
Edinburgh and there is a Beltane network

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:08.940
that provides training for scientists,
for science policymakers to be better

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:14.820
able to build community facilitation
events like that and they have published

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:21.720
a couple of PDFs and books that I could
share with you tied to how to create

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:27.330
those spaces so that you actually build
understanding and it also defines the

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:32.910
differences between things like you know
the benefits and the pros and cons of

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:39.570
what dialogue is, what debate does, what
deliberation does from a social science

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:41.630
perspective.

